## 2. Understanding the Important Form Elements

### 2.1 `<form>`

The `<form>` element groups input controls and provides the mechanism for submitting their data.

**Syntax:**

```html
<form action="/submit" method="POST">
    <!-- Form elements -->
</form>
```

Important attributes:

| Attribute | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `action` | URL where the form data is submitted |
| `method` | HTTP method, commonly `GET` or `POST` |
| `autocomplete` | Controls whether the browser can suggest previously entered values |
| `novalidate` | Disables the browser's automatic validation during submission |
| `target` | Specifies where to display the response |

**GET vs POST:**

| GET | POST |
|---|---|
| Sends data in the URL query string | Sends data in the request body |
| Useful for searches and filters | Commonly used for creating or submitting data |
| Data is visible in the URL | Data is not placed in the URL by default |
| Suitable for bookmarkable requests | Suitable for requests that change server-side state |

Important: POST does not automatically encrypt data. Use HTTPS to protect data in transit.

### 2.2 `<input>`

The `<input>` element creates an interactive field that accepts different kinds of data.

**Syntax:**

```html
<input type="text" id="username" name="username">
```

Common input types:

| Input type | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `text` | Single-line text | Username |
| `password` | Password entry | Login password |
| `email` | Email address | user@example.com |
| `number` | Numeric input | Age |
| `tel` | Telephone number | Mobile number |
| `date` | Date selection | Date of birth |
| `checkbox` | Select one or more options | Accept terms |
| `radio` | Select one option from a group | Gender or plan |
| `file` | Upload a file | Profile picture |
| `range` | Select a value from a range | Volume |
| `color` | Select a color | Theme color |
| `submit` | Submit a form | Submit button |
| `reset` | Reset form controls | Reset button |
| `hidden` | Store a hidden form value | Record identifier |

Example:

```html
<input type="text" placeholder="Enter username">
<input type="email" placeholder="Enter email">
<input type="password" placeholder="Enter password">
<input type="number" placeholder="Enter age">
```

### 2.3 `<label>`

A label describes an input field.

```html
<label for="email">Email Address:</label>
<input type="email" id="email" name="email">
```

The `for` attribute must match the input's `id`.

**Why use labels?**

- Makes forms easier to understand.
- Clicking the label focuses or activates its associated input.
- Improves accessibility for people using assistive technologies.

### 2.4 Buttons inside forms

There are three important button types:

```html
<button type="submit">Submit</button>

<button type="button">Click Me</button>

<button type="reset">Reset</button>
```

| Button type | Behavior |
|---|---|
| `submit` | Submits the form |
| `button` | Does nothing automatically; JavaScript can define its behavior |
| `reset` | Resets form controls to their initial values |

**Important:** A `<button>` inside a form defaults to `type="submit"` if its type is omitted. Specify the type explicitly to avoid accidental submissions.

