## 4. Reading Checkbox and Radio Button Values

Text inputs are not the only controls used in forms. Checkboxes and radio buttons have different behavior.

### 4.1 Checkbox — `.checked`

A checkbox represents an on/off selection.

```html
<label>
    <input type="checkbox" id="terms">
    I accept the terms and conditions
</label>

<button type="button" id="checkButton">Check</button>

<p id="result"></p>

<script>
    const terms = document.getElementById("terms");
    const button = document.getElementById("checkButton");
    const result = document.getElementById("result");

    button.addEventListener("click", function () {

        if (terms.checked) {
            result.textContent = "Terms accepted!";
        } else {
            result.textContent = "Please accept the terms.";
        }

    });
</script>
```

**Important property:**

```javascript
checkbox.checked;
```

It returns a Boolean:

- `true` — checkbox is selected.
- `false` — checkbox is not selected.

### 4.2 Radio buttons

Radio buttons allow the user to select one option from a group.

```html
<label>
    <input type="radio" name="gender" value="Male">
    Male
</label>

<label>
    <input type="radio" name="gender" value="Female">
    Female
</label>

<button type="button" id="showButton">Show Selection</button>

<p id="result"></p>

<script>
    const button = document.getElementById("showButton");
    const result = document.getElementById("result");

    button.addEventListener("click", function () {

        const selected = document.querySelector(
            'input[name="gender"]:checked'
        );

        if (selected) {
            result.textContent = selected.value;
        } else {
            result.textContent = "Please select an option.";
        }

    });
</script>
```

**Important:**

```javascript
document.querySelector('input[name="gender"]:checked');
```

This selects the currently checked radio button in the group.

The radio buttons must share the same `name` to form a group.

