## 5. Form Events in JavaScript

JavaScript provides events that help us respond to user interactions with form controls.

### 5.1 The `input` event

The `input` event fires when the user changes the value of an input through a user interaction.

It is useful for:
- Live character counts
- Search suggestions
- Live form feedback
- Password strength indicators

Example:

```html
<input type="text" id="username" placeholder="Enter your name">

<p id="result"></p>

<script>
    const username = document.getElementById("username");
    const result = document.getElementById("result");

    username.addEventListener("input", function () {

        result.textContent = "You entered: " + username.value;

    });
</script>
```

As the user types, the paragraph updates.

### 5.2 The `change` event

The `change` event fires when a control's value is committed. Exactly when this happens depends on the control type.

For a text input, it generally fires after the value changes and the user leaves the field. For a checkbox or radio button, it fires when the selection changes.

```javascript
const email = document.getElementById("email");

email.addEventListener("change", function () {
    console.log("Email value committed:", email.value);
});
```

### 5.3 The `submit` event

The `submit` event occurs when a form is submitted.

It is generally best to attach the listener to the `<form>` element rather than only to its submit button.

```html
<form id="myForm">

    <input type="text" id="username" name="username">

    <button type="submit">Submit</button>

</form>

<script>
    const form = document.getElementById("myForm");

    form.addEventListener("submit", function (event) {

        event.preventDefault();

        console.log("Form submitted!");

    });
</script>
```

### Why use `event.preventDefault()`?

Normally, submitting a form can cause the browser to navigate to the URL in the form's `action` attribute and send the form data.

When we use:

```javascript
event.preventDefault();
```

we cancel that default submission behavior.

This is useful when we want JavaScript to validate the form or process the data ourselves.

It does not stop event propagation.

### 5.4 `focus` and `blur` events

- `focus`: Fires when an element receives focus.
- `blur`: Fires when an element loses focus.

```javascript
const username = document.getElementById("username");

username.addEventListener("focus", function () {
    console.log("Input focused");
});

username.addEventListener("blur", function () {
    console.log("Input lost focus");
});
```

These events are useful for showing field instructions and validating fields after the user leaves them.

