## 7. HTML5 Built-in Form Validation

HTML provides validation attributes that allow the browser to check common input requirements without writing JavaScript validation code.

### 7.1 `required`

Makes a field mandatory.

```html
<input type="text" name="username" required>
```

The form cannot pass normal browser validation if this field is empty.

### 7.2 `minlength` and `maxlength`

Control the minimum and maximum number of characters for applicable text inputs.

```html
<input
    type="text"
    name="username"
    minlength="3"
    maxlength="15"
    required
>
```

### 7.3 `min` and `max`

Set numeric or date boundaries where supported.

```html
<input type="number" name="age" min="18" max="60">
```

### 7.4 `type="email"`

Checks whether the entered value has a browser-recognized email format.

```html
<input type="email" name="email" required>
```

This does not prove that the email address exists.

### 7.5 `pattern`

Defines a regular expression that the input value must match.

```html
<input
    type="text"
    name="code"
    pattern="[A-Z]{3}[0-9]{3}"
    title="Enter 3 uppercase letters followed by 3 digits"
>
```

Examples of accepted values:

```text
ABC123
XYZ456
```

### 7.6 `step`

Defines the permitted interval between values for applicable input types.

```html
<input type="number" min="0" max="10" step="2">
```

Values such as `0`, `2`, `4`, `6`, `8`, and `10` satisfy the step rule.

### Complete HTML validation example

```html
<form>

    <label for="username">Username:</label>
    <input
        type="text"
        id="username"
        name="username"
        minlength="3"
        maxlength="15"
        required
    >

    <br><br>

    <label for="email">Email:</label>
    <input
        type="email"
        id="email"
        name="email"
        required
    >

    <br><br>

    <label for="age">Age:</label>
    <input
        type="number"
        id="age"
        name="age"
        min="18"
        max="60"
        required
    >

    <br><br>

    <button type="submit">Register</button>

</form>
```

The browser performs its built-in validation when the user tries to submit the form normally.

