## 8. JavaScript Form Validation

HTML validation handles many common requirements. JavaScript is useful when we need custom rules and messages.

For example:
- Password and confirm-password values must match.
- A field must contain a specific combination of characters.
- A username must not contain spaces.
- A custom error message must appear beside a field.

### Example: Basic username validation

```html
<input type="text" id="username" placeholder="Enter username">

<button type="button" id="checkButton">Validate</button>

<p id="message"></p>

<script>
    const username = document.getElementById("username");
    const button = document.getElementById("checkButton");
    const message = document.getElementById("message");

    button.addEventListener("click", function () {

        const value = username.value.trim();

        if (value === "") {
            message.textContent = "Username is required.";
        }
        else if (value.length < 3) {
            message.textContent = "Username must contain at least 3 characters.";
        }
        else {
            message.textContent = "Username is valid!";
        }

    });
</script>
```

### Understanding `.trim()`

```javascript
const value = username.value.trim();
```

The `trim()` method removes whitespace from the beginning and end of a string.

Example:

```javascript
const name = "   Prajwal   ";

console.log(name.trim());
```

Output:

```text
Prajwal
```

Why use it?

Without `trim()`, a user could enter only spaces and the value would not be an empty string.

