## 10. FormData API

JavaScript provides the `FormData` API to collect form fields and their values.

Instead of reading every input individually, you can collect the form's successful controls together.

### Syntax

```javascript
const formData = new FormData(form);
```

Example:

```html
<form id="myForm">

    <input type="text" name="username" value="Prajwal">

    <input type="email" name="email" value="prajwal@example.com">

    <button type="submit">Submit</button>

</form>

<script>

    const form = document.getElementById("myForm");

    form.addEventListener("submit", function (event) {

        event.preventDefault();

        const formData = new FormData(form);

        console.log(formData.get("username"));
        console.log(formData.get("email"));

    });

</script>
```

Output:

```text
Prajwal
prajwal@example.com
```

### Useful FormData methods

| Method | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `get(name)` | Returns the first value for a field name |
| `getAll(name)` | Returns all values for a field name |
| `has(name)` | Checks whether a field name exists |
| `set(name, value)` | Sets a value, replacing existing values for that name |
| `append(name, value)` | Adds another value for that name |
| `delete(name)` | Removes values for a field name |
| `entries()` | Returns key-value pairs |

### Convert FormData into an object

```javascript
const formData = new FormData(form);

const data = Object.fromEntries(formData.entries());

console.log(data);
```

Example output:

```javascript
{
    username: "Prajwal",
    email: "prajwal@example.com"
}
```

**Important details:**

- Form controls need a `name` attribute to be included.
- Disabled controls are not included.
- Unchecked checkboxes and radio buttons are not included.
- Checked checkboxes are included according to their `name` and `value`.
- File inputs produce `File` values.
- `Object.fromEntries()` keeps only one value per property name. For repeated names, use `getAll()` or process the entries appropriately.

### `form.elements`

You can access the form's controls through its `elements` collection.

```javascript
const form = document.getElementById("myForm");

console.log(form.elements["username"]);
console.log(form.elements["email"]);
```

This is useful when working with multiple form controls.

