## 13. Common Mistakes in Form Handling

### Mistake 1: Using `innerHTML` for user input

Incorrect:

```javascript
result.innerHTML = username.value;
```

Why avoid it?

User-controlled content inserted as HTML can create cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities.

Correct:

```javascript
result.textContent = username.value;
```

Use `innerHTML` only when you intentionally need to insert trusted HTML and have handled untrusted content safely.

### Mistake 2: Forgetting `preventDefault()`

Incorrect when handling the form entirely with JavaScript:

```javascript
form.addEventListener("submit", function () {
    console.log("Submitted");
});
```

The browser may navigate or reload as part of normal form submission.

Correct for client-side handling:

```javascript
form.addEventListener("submit", function (event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    console.log("Handled using JavaScript");
});
```

You do not need to prevent the default if you intentionally want the browser to submit the form normally.

### Mistake 3: Using `.value` for checkboxes

Incorrect:

```javascript
if (checkbox.value) {
    console.log("Checked");
}
```

The `value` property represents the value submitted when the checkbox is checked. It does not tell you whether it is selected.

Correct:

```javascript
if (checkbox.checked) {
    console.log("Checked");
}
```

### Mistake 4: Forgetting the `name` attribute

```html
<input type="text" id="username">
```

The input can still be read through JavaScript, but it will not be included in normal form submission or `FormData` under a field name.

Correct:

```html
<input type="text" id="username" name="username">
```

### Mistake 5: Not trimming text input

Incorrect:

```javascript
if (username.value === "") {
    console.log("Required");
}
```

A string containing only spaces passes this check.

Better:

```javascript
if (username.value.trim() === "") {
    console.log("Required");
}
```

### Mistake 6: Attaching submit handling only to the button

A form can be submitted by clicking its submit button or by pressing Enter in certain fields.

Better:

```javascript
form.addEventListener("submit", function (event) {
    event.preventDefault();
});
```

### Mistake 7: Forgetting server-side validation

Browser validation can be bypassed. Always validate important data on the backend before accepting it.

### Mistake 8: Forgetting accessibility

Do not rely only on red text or color to communicate errors. Associate labels and error messages with their inputs, and provide understandable text feedback.

